  <section|Final mathematical corrections to the same-run
  estimate><label|final-mathematical-corrections-to-the-same-run-estimate>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>The Drake separation used in the disk price is max-distance at
    least <verbatim|B_j^2>, not a lower bound on squared max-distance. The
    mode has at least two markers and Borne support placement puts all their
    named origins in the support; the connected-halo tour lemma in the
    centered-construction chapter supplies the cover cost.

    <item>The application assumes <verbatim|B_j\<geq\>8>,
    <verbatim|E_j\<geq\>2>, and separately <verbatim|exp(B_j/64)\<gtr\>2>;
    the last condition is eventually true by the selected scale growth. The
    exponential integrability majorant uses lawfulness' action upper bound,
    the vacuum activity envelope and measurable coefficient fields, together
    with the finite vault and served ledgers. It is not inferred solely from
    compactness of the configuration space.

    <item>Scaling acts on the principal/forced correction families while
    keeping the exponential-entry and marked-activity carriers fixed. The
    witness conditions for <verbatim|Served> are stable, with the envelope
    price multiplied by <verbatim|\|z\|>; the restore operation is not
    asserted to be linear. Downward served transfer is an additional
    hypothesis supplied by the same history's wake, matchup and response
    data; Fubini proves only preservation of mode integrals.

    <item><verbatim|Phi(0)> is the later-rung restoration with its correction
    families set to zero. The opened rung is used to price it after
    transition. The Cauchy consumer's same-run application is expressly
    conditional on branch confluence/credit, pond+geometry, served transfer,
    fixed-rung Cauchy, and compact local Lipschitz receipts. The nonlinear
    one-step response/service prices remain explicit inputs tracked in
    <verbatim|centered-construction>.
  </itemize-minus>

